Dear Mrs. Rainho,

I write to make a formal pre-emptive request for a public, oral hearing in =
AC-2025-LON-004293 once the matter reaches judicial consideration. I make th=
is request at the earliest opportunity in light of my status as a disabled a=
nd medically vulnerable litigant and the subject matter of the claim.

The underlying issues concern HMCTS=E2=80=99s handling of my participation measur=
es, reasonable adjustments and safeguarding needs. These are procedurally co=
mplex matters with significant Equality Act 2010 (ss.20=E2=80=9321) and Public Sec=
tor Equality Duty (s.149) implications, and they cannot be fairly determined=
 on the papers without the Court hearing directly from me.

Given the cumulative administrative failings raised in the claim =E2=80=94 includ=
ing failures in communication, failures to record and apply disability infor=
mation, and the resulting barriers to effective participation =E2=80=94 a paper de=
termination would risk replicating the very exclusion and disadvantage that =
the claim seeks to remedy.

I therefore ask that the matter be listed for a public, oral hearing, for t=
he following reasons:
Equality Act ss.20=E2=80=9321 (reasonable adjustments):
As a disabled and autistic litigant, I require the opportunity to explain, =
in person or via remote oral hearing, the impact of the administrative faili=
ngs on my ability to participate. A paper decision would not meet the statut=
ory requirement to take reasonable steps to avoid substantial disadvantage.
Public Sector Equality Duty (s.149):
The Court is required to have due regard to my disability and vulnerability=
 in determining the appropriate mode of hearing. An oral hearing is necessar=
y to ensure the PSED is properly discharged.
Overriding Objective and Vulnerable Court Users:
The CPR/FPR framework requires the Court to ensure that vulnerable individu=
als can participate effectively. An oral hearing is essential to restore mea=
ningful participation where earlier administrative handling has prevented it=
.
History of procedural barriers:
The claim concerns extensive administrative failings that prevented full pa=
rticipation in earlier proceedings. Determining these matters on the papers =
would risk perpetuating the same barriers.

=20

For these reasons, and to ensure both fairness and compliance with the Cour=
t=E2=80=99s duties, I respectfully request confirmation that the case will be list=
ed for a public, oral hearing when it reaches the judicial stage.

In addition, I must seek an urgent update on the status of my N463 applicat=
ion. You indicated yesterday that the N463 had been referred to the case law=
yer and would be dealt with =E2=80=9Cin due course=E2=80=9D. Given that:
the N463 was filed as an urgent application with a 72-hour turnaround,
I am a disabled and medically vulnerable litigant whose health is deteriora=
ting, and
the underlying claim directly challenges HMCTS=E2=80=99s handling of my participa=
tion measures and reasonable adjustments,
=20

it is essential for me to know how and when this urgent application will be=
 considered.

For clarity, I would be grateful if you could confirm:
Has my N463 application been placed before a judge?
If so, when was this done, and when can I expect a decision?
If not, when today will the application be put before a judge in light of t=
he urgent framework and my medical position?
=20

As noted previously, the Court=E2=80=99s fully engaged duties under the Equality =
Act 2010 (ss.20=E2=80=9321), the Public Sector Equality Duty (s.149) and the overr=
iding objective concerning vulnerable litigants apply to the handling of thi=
s urgent application, not only to the underlying claim. Further delay now ri=
sks compounding the very Equality Act breaches that are at the heart of thes=
e proceedings.

I would be grateful if this email could be treated as a matter of priority =
this morning, and if you could confirm both the intended mode of hearing (pu=
blic, oral hearing) and the precise status and timing of judicial considerat=
ion of the N463.

=20

Kind regards,

Nadia Zahmoul

Email: nadia@rosekross.com

=20

On 02/12/2025, 10:20=E2=80=AFAM, "Nadia" <nadia@rosekross.com> wrote:

=20

Dear Mrs Rainho,

Thank you for your reply and for confirming that my N463 application has be=
en referred to the case lawyer.

I would like to clarify that my request for urgent judicial consideration i=
s made in the context of my status as a disabled and medically vulnerable li=
tigant. I am currently experiencing severe and escalating health deteriorati=
on, and any delay in judicial consideration increases the risk to my health.

=20

As the underlying claim directly concerns HMCTS=E2=80=99s handling of my particip=
ation measures and reasonable adjustments, I respectfully remind the Court t=
hat its fully engaged duties under the Equality Act 2010 (ss.20=E2=80=9321), the P=
ublic Sector Equality Duty (s.149), and the overriding objective concerning =
vulnerable litigants apply to the handling of this N463 request.

=20

I would be grateful if this could be brought to the immediate attention of =
the case lawyer so that my application can be considered with the urgency re=
quired in light of my medical position and statutory protections.

=20

Kind regards,

Nadia Zahmoul

Email: nadia@rosekross.com

